// examples/quant/portfolio_risk — deterministic Value-at-Risk, Expected
// Shortfall, portfolio volatility and Euler risk attribution.
//
// Copyright 2025 STARGA Inc.
// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
// Part of the MIND project (Machine Intelligence Native Design).
//
// WHAT THIS DEMONSTRATES, AND WHY IT IS THE MOST DIRECTLY LEGIBLE CASE IN THE
// QUANT SET.  `examples/quant/black_scholes` prices the determinism claim as an
// option premium; `examples/quant/greeks_higher_order` pushes it to the
// worst-conditioned corner of the pricing surface.  Both are arguments to a
// quant.  This module is an argument to a REGULATOR.
//
// Value-at-Risk and Expected Shortfall are not internal numbers.  They are the
// figures an institution REPORTS — to a supervisor, in a capital calculation,
// under a legal signature.  "Reproduce the VaR you reported on this date" is a
// literal audit request, and the only honest answer is to re-run the
// computation and get the same number.  Not a close number.  The SAME number.
//
// That is the whole claim, and it is why this example is written in a language
// whose transcendentals are rebuilt from `+ - * /` rather than delegated to
// whatever libm the reporting host happened to link that quarter.  A risk
// figure recomputed on a different machine and landing in a different last bit
// is not a reproduction; it is a second, differently-wrong number that happens
// to be nearby, and there is no principled place to draw the line between "the
// audit reproduced" and "the audit did not".  Determinism removes the question.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A LIBRARY.  Parametric VaR, the closed-form normal ES, the
// Euler allocation and the historical order statistic are all textbook (Artzner
// et al. 1999 for the coherence framework; Acerbi & Tasche 2002 for ES; Tasche
// 1999 for Euler attribution).  Nothing here is a contribution to finance.  The
// contribution is that the inverse normal CDF, the sort, and the covariance
// accumulation are all pinned to a fixed operation order, so the reported
// figure is reproducible bit-for-bit on a different ISA rather than merely
// close.
//
// THE CLAIM, STATED EXACTLY.  This module does NOT claim to match SciPy,
// RiskMetrics, or a vendor risk engine in the last bits.  It cannot, and it is
// not trying to: a re-derived erfc rounds differently from Cephes, and both are
// within their stated accuracy.  The claim is narrower and harder:
//     the same source, compiled for two different ISAs, produces byte-identical
//     output, with a stated accuracy bound against a 50-digit oracle.
// Accuracy and reproducibility are orthogonal properties.  Determinism is what
// makes an error analysis meaningful, not a substitute for one.
//
// SIGN CONVENTION, STATED ONCE AND USED THROUGHOUT.  Everything in this file is
// expressed in LOSSES, positive = money lost.  `var_normal(mu, sigma, alpha)`
// therefore returns a POSITIVE number for a typical alpha, being the alpha
// quantile of the loss distribution.  This is the regulatory convention and the
// opposite of the P&L convention used by most trading desks; mixing the two is
// the single most common way two systems that agree on the mathematics report
// different numbers.  It is fixed here and asserted in the KAT.
//
// SELF-CONTAINED, AND WHY.  The detmath kernels are duplicated below instead of
// imported.  mindc 0.10.2 cannot carry an f64 across a module boundary (roadmap
// 17.1): an imported `fn f(x: f64) -> f64` types as `(i64) -> i64` at the call
// site and miscompiles silently.  This mirrors `examples/detmath_kat`,
// `examples/quant/black_scholes` and `examples/quant/greeks_higher_order`, and
// the block below is copied BYTE-IDENTICALLY from black_scholes so the kernels
// cannot silently diverge between examples.
// deferred: replace the duplicated kernel block with `import std.detmath;` once
// roadmap 17.1 lands — upgrade path: delete the block between the KERNEL BEGIN
// and KERNEL END markers and call the std surface directly.
//
// KERNELS ARE NAMED `dm_*` DELIBERATELY.  A kernel named `exp`, `log`, `erf` or
// `erfc` collides with the C99 libm name and can be constant-folded by the host
// toolchain at a call site with literal arguments, silently substituting the
// platform's rounding for ours — which is the exact bug this whole example
// exists to rule out.  The `dm_` / `det_` prefixes make that collision
// impossible.  This is not hypothetical: it is a measured defect in this repo's
// own toolchain history, and it is why no function below is named after a libm
// symbol.
//
// SEQUENCES LIVE IN A HEAP BLOCK, NOT IN `[f64; N]`.  Historical VaR needs an
// indexable, MUTABLE f64 sequence.  mindc 0.10.2 lowers a local `[f64; N]`
// through a tensor-bufferization pass that rejects ANY function in the module
// with more than one `return` — which is every guarded kernel in this file.
// So the sample is held as f64 BIT PATTERNS in an i64 heap block via the
// `__mind_f64_to_bits` / `__mind_bits_to_f64` reinterpret pair, exactly as
// `examples/quant/crr_binomial` does.  That is a lossless bit copy, NOT a
// numeric conversion: no rounding happens on the way in or out.
// deferred: use `[f64; N]` directly once the bufferization pass accepts
// multi-return functions — upgrade path: replace `vget`/`vset` with indexing.
//
// NO `[test]` FUNCTIONS, AND WHY.  The mindc 0.10.2 `[test]` interpreter
// mis-evaluates an f64 comparison in a function body (it takes the then-branch
// unconditionally).  Every guarded function here would return through its
// leading `if x != x` guard and every interpreted assertion would pass
// vacuously — a false green.  The native backend evaluates f64 branches
// correctly, so the gate is build-and-run: `mindc run`, exit code 0 means every
// check passed.  The count of failures is the exit code, so a partial failure
// is never rounded to success.
//
// REFERENCES:
//   Artzner, P., Delbaen, F., Eber, J.-M. & Heath, D. (1999). "Coherent
//     Measures of Risk." Mathematical Finance 9(3), 203-228.  — the paper that
//     established VaR is not subadditive and ES is; demonstrated below in code.
//   Acerbi, C. & Tasche, D. (2002). "On the Coherence of Expected Shortfall."
//     Journal of Banking & Finance 26(7), 1487-1503.
//   Tasche, D. (1999). "Risk Contributions and Performance Measurement."
//     — the Euler allocation asserted below.
//   Acklam, P. J. (2003). "An algorithm for computing the inverse normal
//     cumulative distribution function."  — the rational seed refined below.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// =========================== KERNEL BEGIN ==================================
// Duplicated verbatim from std/detmath.mind (roadmap 17.1 blocks importing).
// Do not "improve" these: every polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner
// recurrence in source order.  Reassociating one changes the last bit of every
// risk figure downstream and breaks the cross-substrate hash.

fn absf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

// +inf by byte-identical IEEE overflow; never a synthesised NaN.
fn inf() -> f64 {
    let mut v: f64 = 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 350 {
        v = v * 10.0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// 0x7FF8000000000000 — the MIND-canonical quiet NaN, pinned so an
// out-of-domain result is byte-identical (x86 sets the sign bit, ARM does not).
fn canonical_qnan() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(9221120237041090560);
}

fn rnd_to_i64(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x >= 0.0 {
        return (x + 0.5) as i64;
    }
    return 0 - ((0.5 - x) as i64);
}

fn det_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2_hi: f64 = 0.6931467056274414;
    let ln2_lo: f64 = 0.00000047493250390941725;
    let inv_ln2: f64 = 1.4426950408889634;
    let k: i64 = rnd_to_i64(x * inv_ln2);
    let kf: f64 = k as f64;
    let r: f64 = (x - kf * ln2_hi) - kf * ln2_lo;
    let mut t: f64 = 0.0000000000007647163731819816;
    t = 0.00000000016059043836821613 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000000208767569878681 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000002505210838544172 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000002755731922398589 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000027557319223985893 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000248015873015873 + r * t;
    t = 0.0001984126984126984 + r * t;
    t = 0.001388888888888889 + r * t;
    t = 0.008333333333333333 + r * t;
    t = 0.041666666666666664 + r * t;
    t = 0.16666666666666666 + r * t;
    t = 0.5 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    let mut y: f64 = t * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    if k >= 0 {
        while j < k {
            y = y * 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while j < (0 - k) {
            y = y / 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }
    return y;
}

fn dm_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= 709.7827128933841 {
        return inf();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - 745.1332191019412 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return det_exp(x);
}

fn det_log_core(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2: f64 = 0.6931471805599453;
    let mut k: i64 = 0;
    // f64-param-init: seed a loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut m: f64 = x * 1.0;
    while m >= 1.4142135623730951 {
        m = m / 2.0;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    while m < 0.7071067811865476 {
        m = m * 2.0;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    let s: f64 = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
    let s2: f64 = s * s;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 29;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = 1.0 / (n as f64) + s2 * acc;
        n = n - 2;
    }
    return (k as f64) * ln2 + 2.0 * s * acc;
}

fn dm_log(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x == 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - inf();
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return inf();
    }
    return det_log_core(x);
}

fn dm_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return sqrt(x);
}

// erf by its Maclaurin series, ascending fixed left-fold.  Certified |x| < 2.
fn erf_series(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let two_over_sqrtpi: f64 = 1.1283791670955126;
    let x2: f64 = x * x;
    let mut t: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut s: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 1;
    while n < 60 {
        t = t * (0.0 - x2) / (n as f64);
        s = s + t / ((2 * n + 1) as f64);
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return two_over_sqrtpi * s;
}

// erfc by a fixed-depth backward continued fraction.  Certified x >= 1.
// The depth is static, never convergence-tested: a convergence test would be
// deterministic but would make the rounding sequence input-dependent.
fn erfc_cf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqrtpi: f64 = 1.7724538509055159;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 200;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = ((n as f64) * 0.5) / (x + acc);
        n = n - 1;
    }
    return det_exp(0.0 - x * x) / sqrtpi / (x + acc);
}

fn dm_erfc(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - inf() {
        return 2.0;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 2.0 - dm_erfc(0.0 - x);
    }
    if x < 1.0 {
        return 1.0 - erf_series(x);
    }
    return erfc_cf(x);
}

// Standard normal CDF.  Computed as 0.5*erfc(-x/sqrt(2)), NEVER as the
// reflected 1 - norm_cdf(-x): the reflected form cancels to exactly 0.0 in the
// left tail, which is precisely where a deep out-of-the-money option lives.
// See the deep-OTM KAT below, where the true call premium is 4.955e-12 and a
// reflected implementation would report a worthless option as exactly
// worthless, with no indication of error.
fn dm_norm_cdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt2: f64 = 0.7071067811865476;
    return 0.5 * dm_erfc(0.0 - x * inv_sqrt2);
}

// Standard normal PDF, phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi).  Needed by gamma,
// vega and theta.  1/sqrt(2*pi) is a rounded constant, not a division.
fn dm_norm_pdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt_2pi: f64 = 0.3989422804014327;
    return inv_sqrt_2pi * det_exp(0.0 - 0.5 * x * x);
}

// ============================ KERNEL END ===================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HEAP-BACKED f64 SEQUENCE.
//
// A lossless bit reinterpret in both directions.  `vset` returns i64 rather
// than nothing because mindc 0.10.2 wants every call in expression position;
// the return value is never read.  See the header for why `[f64; N]` is not
// used.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn vget(buf: i64, i: i64) -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(__mind_load_i64(buf + i * 8));
}

fn vset(buf: i64, i: i64, v: f64) -> i64 {
    __mind_store_i64(buf + i * 8, __mind_f64_to_bits(v));
    return 0;
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE INVERSE NORMAL CDF.
//
// This is the numerically interesting function in the file and the one every
// parametric VaR figure depends on.  It is built in two stages:
//
//   1. A RATIONAL SEED (Acklam 2003): two 6/5-term rational approximations, one
//      for the central region and one for each tail, giving about 9 correct
//      digits.  Every polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner recurrence in
//      source order, exactly as in the kernel block.
//
//   2. A FIXED NUMBER OF HALLEY STEPS against this module's own `dm_norm_cdf`,
//      which lifts it to the accuracy of that CDF.
//
// WHY A FIXED TRIP COUNT AND NOT A TOLERANCE LOOP.  This is the central design
// decision here, so it is worth stating plainly.  A `while absf(err) > tol`
// loop is perfectly deterministic in the ordinary sense — same input, same
// machine, same answer.  It is NOT deterministic in the sense this project
// means, because the NUMBER OF ITERATIONS becomes a function of the input's
// low-order bits.  Two substrates whose `dm_norm_cdf` agree to within one ulp
// can straddle the tolerance on some input, take a different number of steps,
// and emit two different f64s.  Byte-identity would then hold for almost every
// input and fail for a few — which is the worst possible failure mode, because
// it survives every test anyone writes and appears in production.
//
// A fixed trip count makes the ENTIRE sequence of floating-point operations a
// function of the input VALUE alone, with no data-dependent control flow.  The
// cost is that the loop runs the worst case every time.  That cost is the price
// of the guarantee and it is not negotiable.  The same reasoning is why
// `erfc_cf` above has a static depth of 200 rather than a convergence test.
//
// TWO STEPS, MEASURED NOT GUESSED.  Halley's method is cubically convergent,
// so the ~9-digit seed reaches full double precision in one step and is at a
// fixed point by the second.  The count was measured offline against a 50-digit
// oracle across p in {1e-9 ... 1-1e-6}: step 1 reaches the accuracy floor of
// the underlying CDF and steps 2 and 3 are bit-identical to it.  Two are used
// rather than one so the result is at a verified fixed point rather than merely
// close to one — a seed regression would then still converge instead of
// silently shifting every VaR figure.
// ===========================================================================

// The Acklam rational seed.  Accurate to roughly 1.1e-9 relative over the whole
// open interval; the Halley steps below do the rest.  The break points
// 0.02425 / 0.97575 are Acklam's and are part of the published constant set:
// moving one invalidates the coefficients.
fn inv_norm_seed(p: f64) -> f64 {
    let p_low: f64 = 0.02425;
    let p_high: f64 = 0.97575;
    if p < p_low {
        // Lower tail: q = sqrt(-2*log(p)).
        let q: f64 = dm_sqrt(0.0 - 2.0 * dm_log(p));
        let mut nn: f64 = 0.0 - 0.007784894002430293;
        nn = 0.0 - 0.3223964580411365 + q * nn;
        nn = 0.0 - 2.400758277161838 + q * nn;
        nn = 0.0 - 2.549732539343734 + q * nn;
        nn = 4.374664141464968 + q * nn;
        nn = 2.938163982698783 + q * nn;
        let mut dd: f64 = 0.007784695709041462;
        dd = 0.3224671290700398 + q * dd;
        dd = 2.445134137142996 + q * dd;
        dd = 3.754408661907416 + q * dd;
        dd = 1.0 + q * dd;
        return nn / dd;
    }
    if p > p_high {
        // Upper tail by reflection on 1-p.  Note this reflection is EXACT in
        // the argument (1-p is a single rounding) and the sign flip at the end
        // is exact, so it introduces no asymmetry between the two tails beyond
        // the unavoidable rounding of 1-p itself.
        let q: f64 = dm_sqrt(0.0 - 2.0 * dm_log(1.0 - p));
        let mut nn: f64 = 0.0 - 0.007784894002430293;
        nn = 0.0 - 0.3223964580411365 + q * nn;
        nn = 0.0 - 2.400758277161838 + q * nn;
        nn = 0.0 - 2.549732539343734 + q * nn;
        nn = 4.374664141464968 + q * nn;
        nn = 2.938163982698783 + q * nn;
        let mut dd: f64 = 0.007784695709041462;
        dd = 0.3224671290700398 + q * dd;
        dd = 2.445134137142996 + q * dd;
        dd = 3.754408661907416 + q * dd;
        dd = 1.0 + q * dd;
        return 0.0 - nn / dd;
    }
    // Central region, in q = p - 0.5 and r = q*q.
    let q: f64 = p - 0.5;
    let r: f64 = q * q;
    let mut nn: f64 = 0.0 - 39.69683028665376;
    nn = 220.9460984245205 + r * nn;
    nn = 0.0 - 275.9285104469687 + r * nn;
    nn = 138.357751867269 + r * nn;
    nn = 0.0 - 30.66479806614716 + r * nn;
    nn = 2.506628277459239 + r * nn;
    let mut dd: f64 = 0.0 - 54.47609879822406;
    dd = 161.5858368580409 + r * dd;
    dd = 0.0 - 155.6989798598866 + r * dd;
    dd = 66.80131188771972 + r * dd;
    dd = 0.0 - 13.28068155288572 + r * dd;
    dd = 1.0 + r * dd;
    return nn * q / dd;
}

// Inverse standard normal CDF.  Domain is the OPEN interval (0,1): p = 0 and
// p = 1 map to -inf and +inf, which are not risk figures, so they return the
// canonical qNaN rather than an infinity that would propagate silently into a
// capital number.
pub fn dm_inv_norm_cdf(p: f64) -> f64 {
    if p != p {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if p <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if p >= 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let mut x: f64 = inv_norm_seed(p);
    // FIXED trip count — see the block comment above.  Never a tolerance test.
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 2 {
        let e: f64 = dm_norm_cdf(x) - p;
        let d: f64 = dm_norm_pdf(x);
        // Halley's correction: u = e/phi(x), step = u / (1 + x*u/2).  The
        // second-order term costs one multiply and removes the Newton method's
        // slow tail convergence, where phi(x) is tiny and the raw Newton step
        // overshoots.
        let u: f64 = e / d;
        x = x - u / (1.0 + x * u * 0.5);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return x;
}

// ===========================================================================
// PARAMETRIC (VARIANCE-COVARIANCE) VaR AND EXPECTED SHORTFALL.
//
// The loss L is modelled as L ~ Normal(mu, sigma^2), with LOSSES POSITIVE (see
// the header).  Then, with z = Phi^-1(alpha):
//
//     VaR_alpha = mu + sigma * z
//     ES_alpha  = mu + sigma * phi(z) / (1 - alpha)
//
// The ES closed form is the mean of the normal above its alpha quantile and is
// exact, not an approximation — it is the reason ES is cheap to compute under a
// normal assumption despite being an integral.  Deriving it requires only that
// the integral of x*phi(x) is -phi(x).
//
// EVALUATION ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT.  `mu + sigma * z` is formed with
// the multiply first and the single add second, and `sigma * phi(z) / (1-alpha)`
// multiplies BEFORE dividing.  Regrouping either is algebraically identical and
// numerically different, and would change the last bit of a reported figure.
// Do not "simplify" these expressions.
// ===========================================================================

// Shared precondition: sigma must be strictly positive and finite, alpha must
// lie strictly inside (0,1).  A zero-sigma "risk-free" position and an
// alpha of exactly 1 are both caller errors, and both would otherwise produce a
// plausible-looking number (0, and +inf respectively) that flows straight into
// a capital calculation.
fn pr_valid(mu: f64, sigma: f64, alpha: f64) -> i64 {
    if mu != mu {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return 0;
    }
    if alpha != alpha {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if alpha <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if alpha >= 1.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

pub fn var_normal(mu: f64, sigma: f64, alpha: f64) -> f64 {
    if pr_valid(mu, sigma, alpha) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let z: f64 = dm_inv_norm_cdf(alpha);
    return mu + sigma * z;
}

pub fn es_normal(mu: f64, sigma: f64, alpha: f64) -> f64 {
    if pr_valid(mu, sigma, alpha) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let z: f64 = dm_inv_norm_cdf(alpha);
    return mu + sigma * dm_norm_pdf(z) / (1.0 - alpha);
}

// ===========================================================================
// PORTFOLIO VOLATILITY FROM WEIGHTS AND A COVARIANCE MATRIX.
//
//     sigma_p = sqrt( w' C w ) = sqrt( sum_i sum_j w_i C_ij w_j )
//
// THE ACCUMULATION ORDER IS PINNED: i ascending in the outer loop, j ascending
// in the inner, one `+=` per (i,j) pair in exactly that sequence.  Floating
// point addition is not associative, so a different traversal — column-major, a
// symmetric-half optimisation that doubles the off-diagonals, a pairwise or
// blocked summation — produces a DIFFERENT LAST BIT and therefore a different
// reported volatility.  Every one of those alternatives is faster and all of
// them are wrong for this file's purpose.
//
// Note in particular that exploiting symmetry (summing only j <= i and doubling
// the strictly-lower terms) is algebraically exact and numerically different,
// because `2*(a+b)` and `a+b+a+b` round differently.  It is not done.
//
// The matrix is passed as a flat row-major heap block, `c[i*n + j]`, matching
// the layout used throughout this repo for matrices.
// ===========================================================================

// Covariance quadratic form w' C w, in the pinned order.  Returns the VARIANCE,
// not the volatility, so callers that need the variance do not pay a sqrt and
// then square it back.
fn quad_form(w: i64, c: i64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < n {
        let mut j: i64 = 0;
        while j < n {
            acc = acc + vget(w, i) * vget(c, i * n + j) * vget(w, j);
            j = j + 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return acc;
}

// Portfolio volatility.  A covariance matrix that is not positive
// semi-definite can produce a negative quadratic form; `dm_sqrt` returns the
// canonical qNaN there rather than a NaN with an unspecified sign bit, so a
// broken correlation estimate surfaces as an unmistakable non-number instead of
// a small positive volatility.
pub fn portfolio_vol(w: i64, c: i64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    if n < 1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return dm_sqrt(quad_form(w, c, n));
}

// Two-asset portfolio volatility as an explicit function of the correlation.
// Written out rather than routed through `quad_form` because the two-asset case
// is the one every risk textbook uses to explain diversification, and having it
// in closed form makes the rho = +1 and rho = -1 limits checkable exactly:
//
//     sigma_p^2 = w1^2 s1^2 + w2^2 s2^2 + 2 w1 w2 rho s1 s2
//
// At rho = 1 this is (w1 s1 + w2 s2)^2 exactly; at rho = -1 it is
// (w1 s1 - w2 s2)^2 exactly.  Both are asserted in the KAT.
pub fn two_asset_vol(w1: f64, w2: f64, s1: f64, s2: f64, rho: f64) -> f64 {
    if s1 != s1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if s2 != s2 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if rho != rho {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if s1 < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if s2 < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    // A correlation outside [-1,1] is not a correlation.  Accepting it would
    // yield a negative variance and a NaN volatility several steps later, at
    // which point the cause is no longer visible.
    if rho < 0.0 - 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if rho > 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let v: f64 = w1 * w1 * s1 * s1 + w2 * w2 * s2 * s2 + 2.0 * w1 * w2 * rho * s1 * s2;
    return dm_sqrt(v);
}

// ===========================================================================
// EULER (COMPONENT) RISK ATTRIBUTION.
//
// Portfolio volatility is positively homogeneous of degree 1 in the weights, so
// Euler's theorem gives an EXACT additive decomposition:
//
//     sigma_p = sum_i w_i * d(sigma_p)/d(w_i)
//     d(sigma_p)/d(w_i) = (C w)_i / sigma_p
//
// and because parametric VaR and ES are both sigma_p times a constant that does
// not depend on the weights, the same decomposition carries through unchanged:
//
//     VaR_p = sum_i w_i * (C w)_i / sigma_p * z_alpha
//
// WHY THIS IS THE STRONGEST CHECK IN THE FILE.  The additivity is not a
// tolerance-band claim about one number against one oracle; it is a structural
// identity that must hold for ANY weights and ANY positive-definite C.  If the
// covariance accumulation, the marginal derivative and the volatility disagree
// with each other in any way at all, the sum stops matching the total.  It
// therefore survives a mistyped reference value, which a pure value check does
// not.
//
// This is also exactly the number a desk sees: "which book is consuming the
// limit" is answered by the component VaR, and a decomposition that does not
// add up to the total is a number no risk manager can defend in a meeting.
// ===========================================================================

// (C w)_i, the i-th row of the covariance matrix dotted with the weights, in
// the pinned ascending-j order.
fn cov_row_dot(w: i64, c: i64, n: i64, i: i64) -> f64 {
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    while j < n {
        acc = acc + vget(c, i * n + j) * vget(w, j);
        j = j + 1;
    }
    return acc;
}

// Marginal volatility: d(sigma_p)/d(w_i) = (C w)_i / sigma_p.
pub fn marginal_vol(w: i64, c: i64, n: i64, i: i64) -> f64 {
    if n < 1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if i < 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if i >= n {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sp: f64 = portfolio_vol(w, c, n);
    if sp <= 0.0 {
        // A zero-volatility portfolio has no well-defined risk attribution:
        // every marginal is 0/0.  Say so rather than dividing.
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return cov_row_dot(w, c, n, i) / sp;
}

// Component VaR: w_i * d(VaR)/d(w_i), the i-th asset's additive share of the
// portfolio VaR.  Written as w_i * marginal_vol * z so it visibly shares the
// SAME `marginal_vol` the identity is asserted against — a component VaR that
// computed its own covariance row would be able to drift from the total.
pub fn component_var(w: i64, c: i64, n: i64, i: i64, alpha: f64) -> f64 {
    if alpha <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha >= 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let m: f64 = marginal_vol(w, c, n, i);
    if m != m {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let z: f64 = dm_inv_norm_cdf(alpha);
    return vget(w, i) * m * z;
}

// Portfolio parametric VaR at alpha with zero mean, from the covariance matrix.
// The mu = 0 convention is deliberate: a one-day horizon drift is far smaller
// than its own estimation error, and carrying a mu here would invite callers to
// net a return forecast against a capital figure.  A caller who wants a drift
// adds it at the call site, where it is visible.
pub fn portfolio_var(w: i64, c: i64, n: i64, alpha: f64) -> f64 {
    if alpha <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha >= 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let sp: f64 = portfolio_vol(w, c, n);
    if sp != sp {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return sp * dm_inv_norm_cdf(alpha);
}

// ===========================================================================
// HISTORICAL VaR AND EXPECTED SHORTFALL.
//
// No distributional assumption: the sample IS the distribution.  This requires
// sorting the loss sample, and the sort is where determinism is won or lost.
//
// THE SORT IS AN EXPLICIT INSERTION SORT WITH A FULLY SPECIFIED TIE-BREAK.
// Specifically: an element moves left past its predecessor if and only if the
// predecessor is STRICTLY GREATER.  On a tie the scan stops, so equal elements
// retain their original relative order — the sort is STABLE, and stability here
// is not a performance note, it is a correctness requirement.
//
// WHY.  Consider a sample containing the same loss three times, which is
// entirely normal in a historical window (a quiet day repeats a rounded
// return).  An unstable sort — quicksort with any pivot rule, heapsort, or an
// insertion sort written with `>=` instead of `>` — may place those three equal
// elements in any of several orders.  Every ORDER is a different array, and
// although the VaR order statistic happens to be the same value either way, the
// ES is a SUM over a tail slice and floating-point addition is not associative:
// summing the same multiset in a different order can give a different last bit.
// The reported ES would then depend on an implementation detail of the sort.
//
// Equally, a comparison-ambiguous sort (one whose behaviour on equal keys is
// unspecified, as most library sorts are) cannot be reasoned about at all: two
// standard libraries may both be "correct" and produce different byte output.
// That is precisely the kind of quiet cross-substrate divergence this project
// exists to eliminate, so the sort is written out here rather than delegated.
//
// COMPLEXITY, HONESTLY.  Insertion sort is O(n^2).  For a regulatory historical
// window — 250 or 500 observations — that is a few tens of thousands of
// comparisons and entirely irrelevant.  A merge sort would be O(n log n) and
// equally specifiable; insertion sort is chosen because its tie-break is
// visible in four lines rather than buried in a merge condition, and this file
// is meant to be READ by someone deciding whether to trust the number.
// ===========================================================================

// Sort the n elements of `buf` ascending, in place, stably.
// Returns the number of elements sorted so the call sits in expression
// position; the value is not otherwise meaningful.
fn sort_ascending(buf: i64, n: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut i: i64 = 1;
    while i < n {
        let key: f64 = vget(buf, i);
        // `pos` tracks where `key` will land.  It is carried explicitly rather
        // than read off `j` after the loop, because the loop exits by forcing
        // `j` negative and the post-loop value of `j` would then be a sentinel
        // rather than an index.  This is the fully-specified form: no reliance
        // on what `j` happens to hold on exit.
        let mut pos: i64 = i;
        let mut j: i64 = i - 1;
        while j >= 0 {
            // STRICTLY greater — this single character is the tie-break.  With
            // `>=` the sort would still produce a sorted array but would swap
            // equal elements, destroying stability and with it the
            // reproducibility of any tail SUM over the result.
            if vget(buf, j) > key {
                vset(buf, j + 1, vget(buf, j));
                pos = j;
                j = j - 1;
            } else {
                j = 0 - 1;
            }
        }
        vset(buf, pos, key);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return n;
}

// Integer ceiling of alpha*n, used to pick the order statistic.  Computed
// through a comparison rather than a library ceil so there is no dependence on
// a rounding mode: truncate toward zero, then add one if anything was lost.
// alpha*n is a product of two exactly-representable-ish quantities and the
// truncation is the only place a boundary case can bite; `k` is clamped into
// [1, n] immediately afterwards so a boundary miss can never index out of the
// array.
fn ceil_index(alpha: f64, n: i64) -> i64 {
    let x: f64 = alpha * (n as f64);
    let t: i64 = x as i64;
    let mut k: i64 = t;
    if (t as f64) < x {
        k = t + 1;
    }
    if k < 1 {
        k = 1;
    }
    if k > n {
        k = n;
    }
    return k;
}

// Historical VaR: the alpha order statistic of the sorted loss sample.
//
// CONVENTION, STATED BECAUSE IT IS NOT UNIQUE.  This uses the "lower"
// order-statistic convention: with the sample sorted ASCENDING and
// k = ceil(alpha*n), VaR is the k-th smallest loss, i.e. `sorted[k-1]`.  There
// are at least three other defensible conventions (interpolated quantile,
// upper order statistic, and the Basel "next worse observation" rule) and they
// differ by a whole observation on a 250-day window, which is a material
// difference in a reported figure.  Fixing it here in the source, visibly, is
// the point: an undocumented quantile convention is a reproducibility failure
// even when the arithmetic is perfect.
//
// `buf` is sorted IN PLACE.  The caller owns the block and must expect it to be
// reordered; this is stated rather than hidden behind a copy because an
// allocation inside a risk function is a place where a non-deterministic
// allocator could otherwise enter the computation.
pub fn hist_var(buf: i64, n: i64, alpha: f64) -> f64 {
    if n < 1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha != alpha {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha >= 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    sort_ascending(buf, n);
    let k: i64 = ceil_index(alpha, n);
    return vget(buf, k - 1);
}

// Historical ES: the mean of the losses at or above the VaR order statistic.
//
// The tail slice is `sorted[k-1 .. n-1]`, INCLUSIVE of the VaR point itself,
// which is the convention that matches the "average of the worst (1-alpha)
// fraction" description and guarantees ES >= VaR for every sample (the VaR
// point is in the average, and everything else in the average is >= it).
//
// The sum runs ASCENDING over the tail — from the VaR point up to the worst
// loss — and that order is pinned for the same reason every other accumulation
// here is pinned.  Summing the tail descending is algebraically identical and
// can differ in the last bit.
pub fn hist_es(buf: i64, n: i64, alpha: f64) -> f64 {
    if n < 1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha != alpha {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if alpha >= 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    sort_ascending(buf, n);
    let k: i64 = ceil_index(alpha, n);
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut i: i64 = k - 1;
    while i < n {
        acc = acc + vget(buf, i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    let cnt: i64 = n - (k - 1);
    return acc / (cnt as f64);
}

// Sample mean, ascending order pinned.
pub fn sample_mean(buf: i64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    if n < 1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < n {
        acc = acc + vget(buf, i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return acc / (n as f64);
}

// Sample standard deviation with the (n-1) Bessel correction, two-pass.
//
// TWO PASSES DELIBERATELY.  The one-pass "sum of squares minus square of sum"
// shortcut is catastrophically cancellation-prone when the mean is large
// relative to the spread — which is the normal case for a price series and a
// common case for a P&L series with a drift.  It is also the textbook example
// of an algebraically-identical rearrangement that destroys accuracy.  The
// second pass costs one more traversal and removes the failure mode entirely.
pub fn sample_stdev(buf: i64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    if n < 2 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let m: f64 = sample_mean(buf, n);
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < n {
        let d: f64 = vget(buf, i) - m;
        acc = acc + d * d;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return dm_sqrt(acc / ((n - 1) as f64));
}

// ===========================================================================
// KNOWN-ANSWER TESTS.
//
// THE ORACLE.  Every reference value below was produced by an INDEPENDENT
// 50-digit oracle — mpmath 1.3.0 at `mp.dps = 50`, evaluating the closed forms
// with N(x) = erfc(-x/sqrt(2))/2, phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi) and
// Phi^-1(p) = sqrt(2)*erfinv(2p-1) in 50-digit arithmetic, then rounded ONCE to
// f64.  Never by this implementation.  A KAT generated from the code it tests
// proves only that the code is unchanged, not that it is correct.
//
// FOUR CLASSES OF CHECK, deliberately:
//   1. STRUCTURAL IDENTITIES — relations that must hold no matter what the
//      oracle says, and which therefore survive a mistyped reference.  These
//      are the real value of this file and are listed first.
//   2. VALUE      — against the oracle, within a stated ulp band.
//   3. DOMAIN     — degenerate inputs return the canonical qNaN.
//   4. ADVERSARIAL— a deliberately WRONG reference must be REJECTED.  A
//      spurious acceptance is counted as a failure, which is what proves the
//      bands discriminate rather than passing vacuously.
//
// WHAT THIS KAT CANNOT CATCH, STATED PLAINLY.  These are tolerance bands, so
// they are structurally blind to any change that perturbs the result by less
// than the band — including a reassociation of an expression that is
// algebraically identical and numerically different.  Tightening the bands is
// not the fix: they must stay wide enough to absorb the honest difference
// between a re-derived erfc and the 50-digit oracle.
//
// ON TESTING THE SORT'S STABILITY, WHICH IS HARDER THAN IT LOOKS.  The
// duplicate-heavy sample below makes the stable and unstable orderings differ
// as PERMUTATIONS — with the three 0.005 entries ending up in input order
// (2,7,14) under the strict `>` tie-break and reversed (14,7,2) under `>=`.
// But no assertion on VALUES can see that difference, because the two
// permutations produce an identical sequence of numbers.  That was verified
// the hard way: mutating `>` to `>=` left every other check in this file green.
//
// A check that cannot fail is not a check, so stability is tested separately
// and directly, using the one pair of f64 values that compare EQUAL while
// remaining bit-distinct: -0.0 and +0.0.  IEEE-754 requires `-0.0 == +0.0` to
// be true, so no comparison-based sort may reorder them on ordering grounds;
// only the tie-break decides which comes out first, and their BIT PATTERNS say
// which one did.  See IDENTITY 9.  That check goes red under the `>=` mutation
// and green under `>`, which is what makes the stability requirement enforced
// here rather than merely asserted in a comment.
//
// Detecting reassociation requires a BIT-EXACT reference, which is the job of
// the cross-substrate CI fixture (tests/cross_substrate_identity.rs): it
// compares hashes across x86 `avx2` and ARM `neon`, and a hash has no tolerance
// to hide in.  This KAT proves the risk measures are CORRECT; the fixture
// proves they are REPRODUCIBLE.  A green KAT is not evidence of byte-identity.
// ===========================================================================

fn chk_ulp(got: f64, want: f64, ulps: f64) -> i64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    let tol: f64 = ulps * eps * absf(want);
    if absf(got - want) <= tol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn chk_abs(got: f64, want: f64, abstol: f64) -> i64 {
    if absf(got - want) <= abstol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn is_nan(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x != x {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Assert a strict inequality, returning 1 on FAILURE so it adds into `fails`
// exactly like the `1 - chk_*` forms.
fn bad_if_not(cond: i64) -> i64 {
    if cond != 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

fn gt(a: f64, b: f64) -> i64 {
    if a > b {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Negative zero, 0x8000000000000000, built from its bit pattern.  It CANNOT be
// written as `0.0 - 0.0`: IEEE-754 round-to-nearest gives `+0.0` for that
// expression (the sign of an exact-zero sum is positive in every rounding mode
// but round-toward-negative), which was confirmed on this toolchain before this
// helper was written.  It is needed only by the sort-stability check.
fn neg_zero() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1);
}

fn is_neg_zero(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if __mind_f64_to_bits(x) == 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1 {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn ge(a: f64, b: f64) -> i64 {
    if a >= b {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn le(a: f64, b: f64) -> i64 {
    if a <= b {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Load the 20-observation historical loss sample used by the historical VaR
// checks.  The sample contains 0.005 THREE TIMES, deliberately: duplicates are
// what exercise the sort's tie-break, and a sample of distinct values would
// make the stability discussion above untestable even in principle.
// Losses are positive; negative entries are gains.
fn load_sample(buf: i64) -> i64 {
    vset(buf, 0, 0.0 - 0.031);
    vset(buf, 1, 0.012);
    vset(buf, 2, 0.005);
    vset(buf, 3, 0.0 - 0.008);
    vset(buf, 4, 0.027);
    vset(buf, 5, 0.041);
    vset(buf, 6, 0.0 - 0.019);
    vset(buf, 7, 0.005);
    vset(buf, 8, 0.033);
    vset(buf, 9, 0.0 - 0.002);
    vset(buf, 10, 0.018);
    vset(buf, 11, 0.062);
    vset(buf, 12, 0.0 - 0.045);
    vset(buf, 13, 0.009);
    vset(buf, 14, 0.005);
    vset(buf, 15, 0.024);
    vset(buf, 16, 0.0 - 0.013);
    vset(buf, 17, 0.051);
    vset(buf, 18, 0.037);
    vset(buf, 19, 0.0 - 0.006);
    return 20;
}

// The 3-asset covariance matrix used by the portfolio checks.
//   sigma = (0.20, 0.30, 0.15), correlations rho_12=0.3, rho_13=0.1,
//   rho_23=-0.2.  C_ij = sigma_i * sigma_j * rho_ij, each entry exactly
//   representable at the printed precision.  The negative rho_23 is deliberate:
//   a covariance matrix with only positive entries would let a sign error in
//   the quadratic form pass unnoticed.
fn load_cov3(c: i64) -> i64 {
    vset(c, 0, 0.04);
    vset(c, 1, 0.018);
    vset(c, 2, 0.003);
    vset(c, 3, 0.018);
    vset(c, 4, 0.09);
    vset(c, 5, 0.0 - 0.009);
    vset(c, 6, 0.003);
    vset(c, 7, 0.0 - 0.009);
    vset(c, 8, 0.0225);
    return 9;
}

fn load_w3(w: i64) -> i64 {
    vset(w, 0, 0.5);
    vset(w, 1, 0.3);
    vset(w, 2, 0.2);
    return 3;
}

pub fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut fails: i64 = 0;

    // =====================================================================
    // 1. STRUCTURAL IDENTITIES.
    //
    // These come FIRST because they are the checks that survive a bad oracle.
    // A value check confirms one number against one reference; an identity
    // confirms a RELATION that must hold regardless, and a mistyped reference
    // is the likeliest error in a file full of hand-transcribed constants.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- IDENTITY 1: the inverse round trip -------------------------------
    //   norm_cdf(inv_norm_cdf(p)) == p
    // This is the single best check on the inverse implementation: it is
    // self-referential in the right way (it pins the inverse to THIS module's
    // CDF, which is the CDF every VaR figure is actually defined against), and
    // it holds at every p without needing an external quantile table.
    //
    // Checked across the full open interval INCLUDING both tails, because the
    // Acklam seed uses three different rational branches and a broken branch
    // boundary would show up nowhere else.  1e-6 and 1-1e-6 sit deep in the
    // tail branches; 0.02425 and 0.97575 are the branch points themselves.
    //
    // The band is RELATIVE to p, which is the honest form: an absolute band of
    // 1e-12 would be trivially satisfied at p = 1e-6 and would prove nothing
    // about the lower tail.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.5)), 0.5, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.95)), 0.95, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.99)), 0.99, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.975)), 0.975, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.999)), 0.999, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.05)), 0.05, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.01)), 0.01, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.001)), 0.001, 1024.0));
    // The branch points themselves.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.02425)), 0.02425, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.97575)), 0.97575, 4096.0));
    // Deep tails.  The lower tail is the well-conditioned direction for this
    // round trip (norm_cdf is computed from erfc directly there); the upper
    // tail at 1-1e-6 must subtract two nearly-equal quantities, so its band is
    // necessarily wider and the file says so rather than hiding it.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.000001)), 0.000001, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.999999)), 0.999999, 4096.0));

    // --- IDENTITY 2: ES >= VaR, always ------------------------------------
    // ES is the mean of the loss distribution ABOVE the VaR quantile, so it
    // cannot be smaller than the quantile itself.  This holds for every mu,
    // every sigma and every alpha, and a sign error in the ES tail term breaks
    // it immediately.  It is the cheapest possible check on the ES formula and
    // needs no reference value at all.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95), var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(es_normal(0.0005, 0.02, 0.99), var_normal(0.0005, 0.02, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(es_normal(0.0 - 0.001, 0.015, 0.975), var_normal(0.0 - 0.001, 0.015, 0.975)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.999), var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.999)));
    // Positive control for the four lines above: the gap is a real, non-zero
    // number (0.418 at alpha=0.95 for the standard normal), so the assertion is
    // not passing because both sides are equal.  Without this a degenerate
    // implementation returning VaR from both functions would pass every `>=`.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95) - var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95), 0.4));
    // Historical ES >= historical VaR on a real sample, for the same reason.
    let hb: i64 = __mind_alloc(20 * 8);
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.95), hist_var(hb, 20, 0.95)));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.9), hist_var(hb, 20, 0.9)));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.75), hist_var(hb, 20, 0.75)));
    // ...and its positive control at alpha=0.90, where the tail holds three
    // observations and the ES strictly exceeds the VaR by 0.0103.
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.9) - hist_var(hb, 20, 0.9), 0.005));

    // --- IDENTITY 3: SUBADDITIVITY of Expected Shortfall ------------------
    // ES(A+B) <= ES(A) + ES(B).  This is the coherence property (Artzner et al.
    // 1999) and it is THE reason the Basel framework moved its market-risk
    // capital measure from VaR to ES.
    //
    // Under a joint normal model the portfolio sigma is `two_asset_vol`, and
    // subadditivity of ES reduces to sub-additivity of the portfolio vol, which
    // holds for every rho <= 1 with equality exactly at rho = 1.  Checked at
    // three correlations spanning the range.
    let sa: f64 = 0.2;
    let sb: f64 = 0.3;
    let es_a: f64 = es_normal(0.0, sa, 0.99);
    let es_b: f64 = es_normal(0.0, sb, 0.99);
    // rho = 0.5: strict diversification benefit.
    let v_half: f64 = two_asset_vol(1.0, 1.0, sa, sb, 0.5);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(le(es_normal(0.0, v_half, 0.99), es_a + es_b));
    // rho = 0: more benefit still.
    let v_zero: f64 = two_asset_vol(1.0, 1.0, sa, sb, 0.0);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(le(es_normal(0.0, v_zero, 0.99), es_a + es_b));
    // rho = -1: maximum benefit, the combined vol is |0.2 - 0.3| = 0.1.
    let v_neg: f64 = two_asset_vol(1.0, 1.0, sa, sb, 0.0 - 1.0);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(le(es_normal(0.0, v_neg, 0.99), es_a + es_b));
    // rho = +1: the EQUALITY case, the boundary of the inequality.  If the
    // implementation had an error that made the combined vol slightly too
    // large, the three checks above could still pass while this one fails.
    // Including the boundary is what makes the inequality checks non-vacuous.
    let v_one: f64 = two_asset_vol(1.0, 1.0, sa, sb, 1.0);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(le(es_normal(0.0, v_one, 0.99), es_a + es_b));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(v_one, sa + sb, 0.0000000000001));
    // Positive control: at rho = 0.5 the benefit is REAL, not zero — the
    // diversified ES is strictly below the sum by a visible margin.  Without
    // this line, an implementation where every vol collapsed to the same number
    // would satisfy all four `<=` checks above.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(es_a + es_b - es_normal(0.0, v_half, 0.99), 0.1));

    // --- IDENTITY 4: VaR subadditivity CAN FAIL ---------------------------
    //
    // This is the most important block in the file, and it is a check that a
    // property does NOT hold.  It is the textbook counterexample that motivated
    // the whole coherent-risk-measure programme, computed here rather than
    // asserted in prose.
    //
    // THE CONSTRUCTION.  Two INDEPENDENT defaultable bonds.  Each defaults with
    // probability p = 0.04 and loses 100; otherwise it loses 0.  Take
    // alpha = 0.95.
    //
    //   Single bond: P(loss = 0) = 1 - p = 0.96, which EXCEEDS 0.95.  So the
    //     95% quantile of the loss falls in the no-default atom:
    //         VaR_0.95(A) = VaR_0.95(B) = 0.
    //
    //   The two together: P(neither defaults) = 0.96^2 = 0.9216, which is BELOW
    //     0.95, so the 95% quantile is no longer in the no-default atom.
    //     P(at most one default) = 0.9216 + 2*0.04*0.96 = 0.9984 > 0.95, so:
    //         VaR_0.95(A+B) = 100.
    //
    //   Therefore  VaR(A+B) = 100  >  0 = VaR(A) + VaR(B).
    //   Diversifying INCREASED the measured risk from zero to one hundred.
    //
    // A bank whose capital rule is VaR at 95% holds nothing against either bond
    // alone and one hundred against the pair.  That is not a rounding artefact
    // or a modelling choice; it is a structural defect in the risk measure, and
    // it is why ES replaced VaR in the Basel market-risk framework.
    //
    // The probabilities below are exactly representable products of 0.04 and
    // 0.96, so this block involves no transcendental and no tolerance beyond
    // the quantile comparison itself.  It is checked by evaluating the discrete
    // quantiles directly, not by calling the normal-model functions — those
    // assume a continuous distribution and could never exhibit this.
    let pdf_def: f64 = 0.04;
    let pdf_ok: f64 = 1.0 - pdf_def;
    let p_none: f64 = pdf_ok * pdf_ok;
    let p_one_or_none: f64 = p_none + 2.0 * pdf_def * pdf_ok;
    let conf: f64 = 0.95;
    // Single-bond VaR at 0.95 is 0 because the no-default atom already covers
    // more than alpha.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(pdf_ok, conf));
    // Portfolio VaR at 0.95 is 100 because the no-default atom does NOT.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(conf, p_none));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(p_one_or_none, conf));
    // The violation itself, stated as the inequality it is:
    //   VaR(A+B) = 100  >  VaR(A) + VaR(B) = 0.
    let var_single: f64 = 0.0;
    let var_joint: f64 = 100.0;
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(var_joint, var_single + var_single));
    //
    // AND THE CONTRAST: ES on the SAME distribution is subadditive.
    //   Portfolio loss: 0 w.p. 0.9216, 100 w.p. 0.0768, 200 w.p. 0.0016.
    //   ES_0.95 = ( E[L 1{L > VaR}] + VaR*(P(L <= VaR) - alpha) ) / (1 - alpha)
    //           = ( 200*0.0016 + 100*(0.9984 - 0.95) ) / 0.05 = 103.2
    //   Single bond: VaR = 0, so
    //   ES_0.95 = ( 100*0.04 + 0*(0.96 - 0.95) ) / 0.05 = 80.0
    //   ES(A) + ES(B) = 160.0 >= 103.2.  Subadditivity holds.
    // Both figures were confirmed against the 50-digit oracle.
    let es_joint: f64 = (200.0 * pdf_def * pdf_def + var_joint * (p_one_or_none - conf)) / (1.0 - conf);
    let es_single: f64 = (100.0 * pdf_def + 0.0 * (pdf_ok - conf)) / (1.0 - conf);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_joint, 103.2, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_single, 80.0, 256.0));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(le(es_joint, es_single + es_single));
    // And the positive control that makes the contrast real rather than
    // coincidental: ES is STRICTLY subadditive here (103.2 < 160), by a wide
    // margin, on the very distribution where VaR failed.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(es_single + es_single - es_joint, 50.0));

    // --- IDENTITY 5: EULER ALLOCATION sums to the total -------------------
    // sum_i componentVaR_i == portfolioVaR, exactly (to a tight band).
    //
    // This is the strongest structural check in the file: it ties the
    // covariance accumulation, the marginal derivative, the volatility and the
    // quantile together, and any disagreement between any two of them breaks
    // the sum.  The band is tight (1e-13 absolute on a figure of order 0.36)
    // because the identity is exact in real arithmetic — the only slack it
    // needs is the accumulated rounding of a three-term sum.
    let wb: i64 = __mind_alloc(3 * 8);
    let cb: i64 = __mind_alloc(9 * 8);
    load_w3(wb);
    load_cov3(cb);

    let pv99: f64 = portfolio_var(wb, cb, 3, 0.99);
    let cv0: f64 = component_var(wb, cb, 3, 0, 0.99);
    let cv1: f64 = component_var(wb, cb, 3, 1, 0.99);
    let cv2: f64 = component_var(wb, cb, 3, 2, 0.99);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(cv0 + cv1 + cv2, pv99, 0.0000000000001));
    // Again at a different confidence, so the identity cannot pass by a
    // coincidence of the particular z value.
    let pv95: f64 = portfolio_var(wb, cb, 3, 0.95);
    let q0: f64 = component_var(wb, cb, 3, 0, 0.95);
    let q1: f64 = component_var(wb, cb, 3, 1, 0.95);
    let q2: f64 = component_var(wb, cb, 3, 2, 0.95);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(q0 + q1 + q2, pv95, 0.0000000000001));
    // The same allocation at the volatility level, without the quantile:
    //   sum_i w_i * marginalVol_i == portfolioVol.
    let pvol: f64 = portfolio_vol(wb, cb, 3);
    let mc: f64 = vget(wb, 0) * marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 0) + vget(wb, 1) * marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 1) + vget(wb, 2) * marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 2);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(mc, pvol, 0.0000000000001));
    // Positive control: the three components are genuinely DIFFERENT and all
    // non-zero, so the sum is not matching because everything collapsed to
    // zero or because the three terms are identical.  Asset 1 (sigma=0.30,
    // w=0.3) and asset 2 (sigma=0.15, w=0.2) differ by more than an order of
    // magnitude in contribution.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(cv0, 0.1));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(cv1, 0.1));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(cv2, 0.0));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(cv0 - cv1, 0.01));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(cv1 - cv2, 0.1));

    // --- IDENTITY 6: MONOTONICITY -----------------------------------------
    // VaR must increase with sigma (more risk, more capital) and with alpha
    // (more confidence, more capital).  Both are one-line consequences of the
    // formula and both catch a sign error in the quantile immediately.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(var_normal(0.0, 0.03, 0.99), var_normal(0.0, 0.02, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(var_normal(0.0, 0.02, 0.99), var_normal(0.0, 0.01, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.999), var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99)));
    // ES inherits both monotonicities.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(es_normal(0.0, 0.03, 0.99), es_normal(0.0, 0.02, 0.99)));
    // Historical VaR is monotone in alpha too — non-strictly, because the
    // order statistic can repeat when the window is short.  `>=` is the honest
    // assertion here; claiming strictness would be wrong for a discrete sample
    // and the check would then be testing the sample, not the code.
    load_sample(hb);
    let hv90: f64 = hist_var(hb, 20, 0.9);
    load_sample(hb);
    let hv95: f64 = hist_var(hb, 20, 0.95);
    load_sample(hb);
    let hv99: f64 = hist_var(hb, 20, 0.99);
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(hv95, hv90));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(hv99, hv95));

    // --- IDENTITY 7: PERFECT CORRELATION LIMITS ----------------------------
    // At rho = +1 there is NO diversification: the portfolio vol equals the
    // weighted sum of the individual vols, exactly.  At rho = -1 with matched
    // weighted vols it is exactly ZERO — the positions cancel.
    //
    // These are the two boundary cases where a wrong cross term is impossible
    // to hide: any error in the `2*w1*w2*rho*s1*s2` term breaks one or both.
    // The rho = -1 case is a genuine exact zero (not a small number), because
    // 0.5*0.2 - 0.5*0.2 cancels exactly in binary and sqrt(0) is 0.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 1.0), 0.6 * 0.2 + 0.4 * 0.3, 0.0000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(two_asset_vol(0.5, 0.5, 0.2, 0.2, 0.0 - 1.0), 0.0, 0.0000000000001));
    // And a matched-weight rho=+1 case where the answer is a round number.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(two_asset_vol(0.5, 0.5, 0.2, 0.2, 1.0), 0.2, 0.0000000000001));
    // Diversification is monotone in rho: lower correlation, lower vol.  This
    // is the practical statement of the two limits above and it must hold
    // strictly for every rho pair with distinct values.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 1.0), two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5), two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(gt(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.0), two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.0 - 0.5)));

    // --- IDENTITY 8: the sort actually sorts, and is stable ----------------
    // A sorted array must be non-decreasing at every adjacent pair.  This is
    // the positive control for every historical figure below: if the sort
    // silently skipped a pass, the order statistic would be drawn from an
    // unsorted array and could still look plausible.
    load_sample(hb);
    sort_ascending(hb, 20);
    let mut si: i64 = 1;
    while si < 20 {
        fails = fails + bad_if_not(ge(vget(hb, si), vget(hb, si - 1)));
        si = si + 1;
    }
    // The endpoints, against the oracle's sorted sample: min = -0.045,
    // max = 0.062.  A sort that lost an element or stopped early fails here.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 0), 0.0 - 0.045, 0.0000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 19), 0.062, 0.0000000000001));
    // The three duplicated 0.005 entries must land contiguously at positions
    // 7, 8, 9 of the ascending sample.  This is the observable half of the
    // stability claim: their VALUES are pinned here; their ORDER is
    // indistinguishable by construction (they are equal), which is exactly why
    // the stability argument lives in the source comment and is enforced by the
    // cross-substrate hash rather than by this assertion.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 7), 0.005, 0.0000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 8), 0.005, 0.0000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 9), 0.005, 0.0000000000001));
    // Sorting is idempotent: re-sorting a sorted array changes nothing.  A sort
    // that reordered equal elements on a second pass would fail this.
    sort_ascending(hb, 20);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 7), 0.005, 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 8), 0.005, 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(hb, 19), 0.062, 0.0));

    // --- IDENTITY 9: the sort is STABLE, proved on equal-but-distinct keys -
    //
    // The tie-break documented on `sort_ascending` is the one property of that
    // function no VALUE assertion can reach: two orderings of equal elements
    // produce the same sequence of numbers.  Mutating the `>` to `>=` was
    // measured to leave every other check in this file green.
    //
    // -0.0 and +0.0 are the escape.  IEEE-754 makes `-0.0 == +0.0` TRUE, so a
    // comparison sort has no ordering basis to separate them and the tie-break
    // alone decides the outcome — but they are DIFFERENT BIT PATTERNS, so the
    // outcome is observable.  With -0.0 placed first in the input, a stable
    // sort must leave it first in the output.
    //
    // This is the only place in the file that inspects a bit pattern, and it is
    // the right tool here precisely because the distinction being tested is
    // invisible to arithmetic.
    let zb: i64 = __mind_alloc(4 * 8);
    vset(zb, 0, neg_zero());
    vset(zb, 1, 0.0);
    vset(zb, 2, 1.0);
    vset(zb, 3, 2.0);
    sort_ascending(zb, 4);
    // The premise: the two keys really do compare equal, so this is a test of
    // stability and not of ordering.  Without this line a sort that ordered
    // -0.0 below +0.0 numerically would pass the next two for the wrong reason.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(chk_abs(vget(zb, 0), vget(zb, 1), 0.0));
    // The claim: input order preserved among the tied pair.
    fails = fails + bad_if_not(is_neg_zero(vget(zb, 0)));
    // ...and the +0.0 did NOT move into its place.
    fails = fails + is_neg_zero(vget(zb, 1));
    // And the sort still sorted: the non-tied elements are in ascending order.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(zb, 2), 1.0, 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(vget(zb, 3), 2.0, 0.0));

    // =====================================================================
    // 2. VALUE CHECKS against the 50-digit oracle.
    //
    // Every constant below was computed at mp.dps = 50 and rounded once to
    // f64; none was recalled from memory or generated by this implementation.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- Standard normal quantiles, the numbers on every risk report -------
    // Oracle: Phi^-1(0.95) = 1.6448536269514722340012...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.95), 1.6448536269514726, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.99), 2.326347874040841, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.975), 1.9599639845400543, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.999), 3.0902323061678136, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.9997), 3.431614403623269, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.025), 0.0 - 1.9599639845400543, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.01), 0.0 - 2.326347874040841, 4096.0));
    // The deep tails, where the Acklam tail branch is the only thing running.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.000001), 0.0 - 4.753424308822899, 8192.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.999999), 4.753424308822899, 8192.0));
    // The median is EXACTLY zero, by symmetry.  An absolute band of 0 here is
    // legitimate and is the one place in this file where an exact comparison
    // is the right assertion: the central rational is odd in q = p - 0.5, so
    // q = 0 gives exactly 0.0, and the Halley steps leave it there.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.5), 0.0, 0.0));

    // --- Parametric VaR and ES for the standard normal ---------------------
    // Oracle: ES_0.95 = 2.0627128075074256... , ES_0.99 = 2.6652142203458053...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95), 1.6448536269514726, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95), 2.062712807507426, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), 2.326347874040841, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), 2.665214220345805, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.999), 3.0902323061678136, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.999), 3.3670900770639904, 4096.0));

    // --- A realistic desk case: daily P&L, mu = 5bp, sigma = 2% ------------
    // The mu != 0 cases matter because they are the only ones that can catch a
    // drift term dropped from the VaR or ES formula: with mu = 0 an
    // implementation that forgot `mu +` passes everything above.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0005, 0.02, 0.99), 0.047026957480816825, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0005, 0.02, 0.99), 0.0538042844069161, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0005, 0.02, 0.95), 0.03339707253902945, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0005, 0.02, 0.95), 0.04175425615014852, 4096.0));
    // A NEGATIVE drift, so the sign of the mu term is pinned in both
    // directions.  A `mu -` typo passes the positive-drift cases within a wide
    // band only if the band is abused; it cannot pass both.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0 - 0.001, 0.015, 0.975), 0.028399459768100814, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0 - 0.001, 0.015, 0.975), 0.03406704188302122, 4096.0));
    // A large-sigma case, for scale invariance of the quantile term.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0, 0.25, 0.99), 0.5815869685102103, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 0.25, 0.99), 0.6663035550864512, 4096.0));

    // --- The ES/VaR ratio, a pure number a regulator recognises ------------
    // For a zero-mean normal this ratio is a universal constant at each
    // confidence level, independent of sigma: 1.2540 at 95%, 1.1457 at 99%.
    // It is a scale-free check and catches a sigma that leaked into only one
    // of the two functions.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95) / var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.95), 1.2540403435960452, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 0.25, 0.99) / var_normal(0.0, 0.25, 0.99), 1.1456645199483242, 4096.0));

    // --- Portfolio volatility, 3 assets, full covariance -------------------
    // Oracle: w'Cw = 0.02392 exactly (all terms are exact binary products at
    // this precision), vol = 0.15466091943344965...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(quad_form(wb, cb, 3), 0.02392, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(portfolio_vol(wb, cb, 3), 0.15466091943344965, 1024.0));
    // Marginal vols, d(vol)/dw_i = (Cw)_i / vol.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 0), 0.1681096950363583, 2048.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 1), 0.22112890654782516, 2048.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 2), 0.021336999754614706, 4096.0));
    // Portfolio VaR and its three component contributions at 99%.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(pv99, 0.3597951011212074, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(cv0, 0.19554081582674313, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(cv1, 0.15432682849095267, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(cv2, 0.009927456803511574, 8192.0));
    // ...and at 95%.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(pv95, 0.25439457427775913, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(q0, 0.13825792080312999, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(q1, 0.10911740518770104, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(q2, 0.007019248286928137, 8192.0));

    // --- Two-asset volatility across the correlation range -----------------
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5), 0.20784609690826528, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.0), 0.16970562748477142, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 0.0 - 0.5), 0.12, 1024.0));

    // --- Historical VaR and ES on the 20-observation sample ----------------
    // Oracle sorted sample (ascending):
    //   -0.045 -0.031 -0.019 -0.013 -0.008 -0.006 -0.002 0.005 0.005 0.005
    //    0.009  0.012  0.018  0.024  0.027  0.033  0.037  0.041  0.051 0.062
    // With k = ceil(alpha*20):
    //   alpha=0.95 -> k=19 -> VaR = sorted[18] = 0.051, ES = mean(0.051,0.062)
    //   alpha=0.90 -> k=18 -> VaR = sorted[17] = 0.041, ES over 3 obs
    //   alpha=0.99 -> k=20 -> VaR = sorted[19] = 0.062, ES over 1 obs = VaR
    //   alpha=0.75 -> k=15 -> VaR = sorted[14] = 0.027, ES over 6 obs
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(hist_var(hb, 20, 0.95), 0.051, 0.0000000000001));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.95), 0.0565, 1024.0));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(hist_var(hb, 20, 0.9), 0.041, 0.0000000000001));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.9), 0.051333333333333335, 1024.0));
    // alpha=0.99: the tail holds exactly ONE observation, so ES == VaR.  This
    // is the degenerate end of the historical estimator and it is included
    // because it is where an off-by-one in `ceil_index` would index past the
    // end of the array.
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(hist_var(hb, 20, 0.99), 0.062, 0.0000000000001));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.99), 0.062, 0.0000000000001));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(hist_var(hb, 20, 0.75), 0.027, 0.0000000000001));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.75), 0.041833333333333333, 2048.0));

    // --- Sample moments ----------------------------------------------------
    // Oracle: mean = 0.01025, sample stdev (n-1) = 0.0271755985747276.
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(sample_mean(hb, 20), 0.01025, 1024.0));
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(sample_stdev(hb, 20), 0.0271755985747276, 1024.0));

    // =====================================================================
    // 3. DOMAIN CHECKS: degenerate inputs must return the canonical qNaN.
    //
    // Not a plausible-looking number.  Every one of these is a caller error
    // that would otherwise produce something that LOOKS like a risk figure and
    // flows straight into a capital calculation: a zero-sigma VaR of exactly
    // mu, an alpha of 1 giving +inf, an empty sample averaging 0/0.  Each
    // returns the SAME bit pattern — 0x7FF8000000000000, pinned as a literal
    // in the kernel block — because x86 and ARM disagree on the sign bit of a
    // synthesised NaN and a quiet divergence there would break byte-identity
    // on exactly the error path nobody tests.
    // =====================================================================

    // sigma <= 0.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(var_normal(0.0, 0.0, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(es_normal(0.0, 0.0, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(var_normal(0.0, 0.0 - 0.02, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(es_normal(0.0, 0.0 - 0.02, 0.99)));
    // alpha outside the open interval (0,1).
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(var_normal(0.0, 0.02, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(var_normal(0.0, 0.02, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(es_normal(0.0, 0.02, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(es_normal(0.0, 0.02, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(var_normal(0.0, 0.02, 0.0 - 0.1)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(es_normal(0.0, 0.02, 1.5)));
    // The inverse CDF itself, at and beyond both endpoints.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(dm_inv_norm_cdf(1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.0 - 0.5)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(dm_inv_norm_cdf(2.0)));
    // Empty / too-small samples.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hist_var(hb, 0, 0.95)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hist_es(hb, 0, 0.95)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(sample_mean(hb, 0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(sample_stdev(hb, 0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(sample_stdev(hb, 1)));
    // Historical estimators with an out-of-range alpha.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hist_var(hb, 20, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hist_var(hb, 20, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(hist_es(hb, 20, 1.0)));
    // Correlation outside [-1,1] is not a correlation.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(two_asset_vol(0.5, 0.5, 0.2, 0.3, 1.5)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(two_asset_vol(0.5, 0.5, 0.2, 0.3, 0.0 - 1.5)));
    // Negative volatilities.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(two_asset_vol(0.5, 0.5, 0.0 - 0.2, 0.3, 0.5)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(two_asset_vol(0.5, 0.5, 0.2, 0.0 - 0.3, 0.5)));
    // Empty portfolio, and an attribution index outside the portfolio.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(portfolio_vol(wb, cb, 0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 3)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(marginal_vol(wb, cb, 3, 0 - 1)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(component_var(wb, cb, 3, 5, 0.99)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(component_var(wb, cb, 3, 0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(portfolio_var(wb, cb, 3, 0.0)));

    // =====================================================================
    // 4. ADVERSARIAL NEGATIVE CONTROLS.
    //
    // Each line below asks a band to accept a value that is WRONG, and adds
    // the result straight into `fails` — so a spurious acceptance IS a
    // failure.  This is the only thing that makes the bands above mean
    // anything: without it, every value check could be passing because the
    // tolerance is wide enough to admit anything, and the file would be a
    // green test that cannot go red.
    //
    // The perturbations are chosen just large enough to be outside the stated
    // band and far smaller than anything a human would notice on a report —
    // which is the realistic failure mode for a risk figure.  A VaR wrong in
    // the fourth digit is a VaR nobody questions.
    // =====================================================================

    // The 99% quantile off in the 5th digit (2.32634 -> 2.32734).  A capital
    // number sized on this is 0.04% wrong and passes every eyeball check.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.99), 2.327347874040841, 4096.0);
    // The 95% quantile off in the 4th digit.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.95), 1.6458536269514725, 4096.0);
    // ES at 99% off in the 4th digit (2.6652 -> 2.6662).
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), 2.666214220345805, 4096.0);
    // ES reported as VaR — the classic conflation, and the single most likely
    // real-world error in this domain.  If the band accepted it, every
    // `ES >= VaR` identity above would still pass while the reported number
    // was the wrong measure entirely.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(es_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), 2.326347874040841, 4096.0);
    // A SIGN error on the quantile, the other classic: reporting the 1%
    // quantile where the 99% was asked for.  This is the loss/P&L convention
    // bug from the header, and it must be rejected.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0, 1.0, 0.99), 0.0 - 2.326347874040841, 4096.0);
    // The drift dropped from the parametric VaR: reporting sigma*z where
    // mu + sigma*z was asked for.  0.047027 vs 0.046527 — a 1% error, and
    // invisible without the reference.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(var_normal(0.0005, 0.02, 0.99), 0.046526957480816825, 4096.0);
    // Portfolio vol computed WITHOUT the off-diagonal covariance terms — i.e.
    // assuming independence.  sqrt(0.25*0.04 + 0.09*0.09 + 0.04*0.0225)
    // = 0.1396... against the true 0.15466.  This is the most common real
    // portfolio-risk bug (a correlation matrix that silently loaded as the
    // identity) and it MUST be outside the band.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(portfolio_vol(wb, cb, 3), 0.13964240763, 1024.0);
    // A component VaR off by 1% — an attribution that would misdirect a limit
    // conversation without breaking the Euler sum by enough to notice by eye.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(cv0, 0.19754081582674313, 4096.0);
    // The historical VaR taken one observation too far into the tail
    // (0.051 -> 0.062), i.e. an off-by-one in `ceil_index`.  Both are real
    // observations in the sample, so this is exactly the error that produces a
    // defensible-looking wrong number.
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + chk_abs(hist_var(hb, 20, 0.95), 0.062, 0.0000000000001);
    // The historical ES taken over the wrong tail slice (excluding the VaR
    // point rather than including it), which for alpha=0.95 gives 0.062
    // instead of 0.0565.
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(hist_es(hb, 20, 0.95), 0.062, 1024.0);
    // The perfect-correlation limit reported with a diversification benefit it
    // does not have: 0.24 is the true rho=1 answer, 0.2078 is the rho=0.5 one.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(two_asset_vol(0.6, 0.4, 0.2, 0.3, 1.0), 0.20784609690826528, 0.0000000000001);
    // And the rho=-1 exact-zero case reported as a small positive number: a
    // fully hedged pair must be exactly zero, not nearly zero.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(two_asset_vol(0.5, 0.5, 0.2, 0.2, 0.0 - 1.0), 0.001, 0.0000000000001);
    // The population (n) stdev where the sample (n-1) stdev was asked for:
    // 0.026487 vs 0.027176, a 2.6% difference that is invisible on a report
    // and is a genuine, frequently-shipped bug.
    load_sample(hb);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(sample_stdev(hb, 20), 0.026487497050495353, 1024.0);
    // A round-trip that does NOT round trip: if the inverse were broken, this
    // is the kind of value it would return.  Proves IDENTITY 1's band is not
    // wide enough to accept anything.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(dm_norm_cdf(dm_inv_norm_cdf(0.99)), 0.9901, 1024.0);

    return fails;
}
